title: 'fix(xcm): charge precise barrier-check weight on barrier rejection'
doc:
- audience: Runtime Dev
  description: |-
    When a barrier rejects an XCM, the executor previously reported the full message weight as
    `used`, over-estimating the work actually done. This adds an optional
    `WeightBounds::barrier_check_weight()` hook (default `None`): when a weigher provides it, the
    executor charges that precise weight on barrier rejection; otherwise it falls back to the
    previous full-message-weight behaviour, so existing `XcmConfig`s are unaffected.

    Two benchmarks are added to `pallet_xcm_benchmarks::generic` to measure that weight:
    `barrier_check_ref_time` and `barrier_check_proof_size` (driven by new
    `worst_case_barrier_check_ref_time()` / `worst_case_barrier_check_proof_size()` config
    methods). A barrier's worst case can have disjoint paths that are each worst in a different
    weight dimension (e.g. a compute-heavy origin-descent path and a storage-reading
    query-response path that no single message can combine), so each path is benchmarked on its
    own and the runtime combines the two results with a component-wise `Weight::max` — a safe and
    tight bound over either path. A runtime whose barrier has a single message that is worst in
    both dimensions can implement just one of the two config methods and use that benchmark alone.
    Westend and the Westend system parachains (Asset Hub, Bridge Hub, Collectives, Coretime,
    People) wire their executor `Weigher` to report the benchmarked value.

    Closes #7965.
